clean_summary:
If the world as we see it, including ourselves, is the answer, then what is the question? This reflects on tuning the hyperparameters of the universe across many independent runs and what fitness function should be used to discover us or something more interesting. At @FLAIR_ox, there has been ongoing discussion around this question, leading to a practical attempt at providing an answer.

While intriguing results have been obtained, the current approach may still rely too much on amortized human priors, which could affect robustness in long-term, high-compute optimization. The search for the question continues.

@uljadb99: "Natural evolution's open-endedness leads to beautiful, complex emergent structures and self-organizing behavior. Replicating this in silico is famously hard. Our paper points to a promising direction by evolving populations of competing neural cellular automata with lifelike behavior."

We can now interact with Gemini in real-time inside Genie worlds. Congratulations to @ashrewards and team on this exciting new direction.

@binks_stewart: "Project Genie is magical, but we've also been working on some new ways to interact with another player or agent. It was super fun to demo this at Google I/O, where attendees explored worlds with Gemini as a companion. We are excited to see how this can enable Gemini to learn how to interact with humans in embodied environments."

Examples of interacting with Gemini in these generated worlds include a video demonstration.
